        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $S_2$, $S_3$ and $S_6$ be the sums of the multiples of 2, 3 and 6 respectively, up to 1000. There are 500 multiples of 2, 333 multiples of 3 and 166 multiples of 6.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}S_2&=\\frac{500}{2}(2+1000)\\\\S_2&=250\\,500\\\\S_3&=\\frac{333}{2}(3+999)\\\\S_3&=166\\,833\\\\S_6&=\\frac{166}{2}(6+996)\\\\S_6&=83\\,166\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe multiples of 6 have been counted twice, so subtract their sum once.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}S&=250\\,500+166\\,833-83\\,166\\\\S&=334\\,167\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
